<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="p" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="-1 0 1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="q" from="-4" to="4" step="1" exclude="-1 0 1 $p -$p"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="r" from="-4" to="4" step="0.25" exclude="-1 0 1 $p -$p $q -$q"/>
  
  <function name="f">$p*x^2 + $q*x + $r</function>
  <function name="fP">2*$p*x + $q</function>
  <function name="fPP">2*$p</function>
  <function name="T2">$b0 + $b1*x + $b2*x^2</function>
  <function name="T2P">$b1 + 2*$b2*x</function>
  <function name="T2PP">2*$b2</function>
  <function name="T2Symb">b_0 + b_1*x + b_2*x^2</function>
  <function name="T2PSymb">b_1 + 2*b_2*x</function>
  <function name="T2PPSymb">2b_2</function>

  <math name="a">0</math>
  <math name="b0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="b1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="b2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
</setup>

<p>
  Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function whose quadratic approximation at <m>x = 0</m> is 
  <me>
    T_2(x) = $f
  </me> 
  which has the standard property that <m>T_2(x)</m> has the same height, slope, and concavity as <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x = 0</m>. 
</p>


<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the following values of <m>T_2(x)</m>.  
      </p>    
      <p>
        <m>T_2(0) = </m> <answer>$r</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_2'(0) = </m> <answer>$q</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_2''(0) = </m> <answer>2*$p</answer>
      </p>

    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        In addition, determine the following values of <m>f(x)</m>.  
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f(0) = </m> <answer>$r</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f'(0) = </m> <answer>$q</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f''(0) = </m> <answer>2*$p</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine a formula for the tangent line approximation, <m>L(x)</m>, to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>L(x) = </m><answer>$q*x + $r</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Complete the following sentence to describe how the tangent line approximation compares to the true value of <m>f(x)</m> near <m>x = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>f''(0)</m> is <choiceInput name="fPPsign" inline>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>negative</choice>
          <choice>zero</choice>
          <choice>positive</choice>
        </choiceInput>, it follows that near <m>x = 0</m>,
        <m>f(x)</m> is <choiceInput name="fconcav" inline>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>concave down</choice>
          <choice>linear</choice>
          <choice>concave up</choice>
        </choiceInput>, so the tangent line to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m> lies
        <choiceInput name="abovebelow" inline>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>below</choice>
          <choice>above</choice>
        </choiceInput> the curve, and thus the approximation <m>L(x)</m> has values that
        <choiceInput name="bigsmall" inline>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>underestimate</choice>
          <choice>overestimate</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        the true value of <m>f(x)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          ($b2 > 0 and $fPPsign.selectedIndices=4 and $fconcav.selectedIndices=4 and $abovebelow.selectedIndices=2 and $bigsmall.selectedIndices=2)
          or
          (0 > $b2 and $fPPsign.selectedIndices=2 and $fconcav.selectedIndices=2 and $abovebelow.selectedIndices=3 and $bigsmall.selectedIndices=3) 
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>